2026-11-10T20:47:23.0129872Z <details><summary>How to read it, and what it cannot show</summary>
2026-11-10T20:47:23.0130155Z 
2026-11-10T20:47:23.0131369Z - **The four comparisons the assessment reads,** declared in bench/report.py (`assessment`): best against best (wall), CPU per byte at the most threads, amplification at each thread count, and peak RSS at the most threads. Each moves only where its 95% interval excludes no change. With no real change, about one run in five flags one by chance: a flag that matters reproduces on a re-run.
2026-11-10T20:47:23.0133506Z - **A trade-off is not a failure.** Faster for more CPU or memory, or the reverse, is for the reviewer to weigh against the deployment; the report names it, and nothing here fails CI but the outputs and the accounting.
2026-11-10T20:47:23.0135309Z - **What it can't show:** which shared resource a rise in amplification comes from (cache, memory bandwidth, SMT, the scanner's handoffs): no hardware counters are read. CI's runners have 2 physical cores, so it cannot show a knee past 2 (the workstation's is at 4-5 workers, #39). The cores' L3 is shared with other tenants and its size unknown.
2026-11-10T20:47:23.0137901Z - **Run-queue wait** (threads' mean count runnable, not running; schedstat): base 1t 0.00, base 2t 0.75, base 4t 1.17, head 1t 0.00, head 2t 0.75, head 4t 1.16. A pinned 2-thread run has four threads (two workers, the scanner, the reader) on two CPUs, so some wait is its shape, not interference.
